  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM EDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nAnother day with a largely persistence forecast as midlevel ridge \nand associated surface anticyclone remain locked over the Great \nLakes. New England upper low finally opens up and lifts toward the \nCanadian Maritimes tomorrow allowing our ridge to drift eastward and \nsouthwest return flow to increase by late in the day. One more \nclear/calm night first though with lows in the upper 40s to around \n50F. Patchy fog is possible once again but crossover temps may be a \nlittle harder to exceed than previous nights. Not expecting any \nwidespread, dense fog but nearly ideal radiational cooling always \nraises the possibility for some localized impacts as we have seen \nthe past two days.\n\nAttention then turns to the northern stream Pacific jet currently \nentering British Columbia and the upper low digging through \nCalifornia. The eastward migration of these two features will drive \nour sensible weather through the second half of the week. The \ninitial cold front still looks to stall to our northwest on Wed as \nit becomes increasingly divorced from the leading midlevel shortwave \nand strong southwest flow develops in the Upper Midwest downstream \nof ejecting upper low. Can't entirely rule out a stray shower in our \nfar NW Wed evening but by and large expect one more day of dry and \npleasant weather with highs near 80F. This front doesn't really \ncross our area until late Thu, possibly extending into Fri, as we \nawait the arrival of a secondary, much more potent northern stream \ntrough. Good 850mb theta-e will be pumped into the region ahead of \nthe southern stream upper low by Thu but surface dewpoints are still \nonly in the low/mid 60s with poor lapse rates aloft yielding very \nmeager instability profiles. This is especially true if precip holds \noff until Thu night as is currently trending. Some embedded \nthunderstorms certainly possible but severe weather is not expected. \nMarginal instability will aid rain coverage/intensity but without a \nstrong convective element expect more of a widespread, moderate rain \nevent. Rain amounts around an inch are likely for most with pockets \nof 1.5\" possible. Given recent stretch of dry weather, our area \nshould be able to tolerate that with minimal impacts. Still some \nuncertainty with the weekend forecast and whether or not this \nfrontal boundary completely clears our CWA as remnant upper low/ \nmoisture plume slowly eject eastward. No changes to concensus \ninitialization here.",
